 "chapter": "20",
 "title": "Amines and Amides",
 "short": "Amines and Amides",
 "lead": "Nitrogen added to an organic framework gives two families of compounds: <span data-concept=\"amine\">amines</span>, in which nitrogen bonds to carbon or hydrogen atoms and keeps a lone pair of electrons, and <span data-concept=\"amide\">amides</span>, in which nitrogen bonds to the carbon atom of a <span data-concept=\"carbonyl-group\">carbonyl group</span>. The section follows them into DNA, the alkaloids, proteins, and Kevlar.",
 "objectives": [
  "Describe the structure and properties of an amine",
  "Describe the structure and properties of an amide"
 ],
 "summary_html": "<p>The addition of nitrogen into an organic framework leads to two families of molecules. Compounds containing a nitrogen atom bonded in a hydrocarbon framework are classified as <span data-concept=\"amine\">amines</span>. Compounds that have a nitrogen atom bonded to one side of a <span data-concept=\"carbonyl-group\">carbonyl group</span> are classified as <span data-concept=\"amide\">amides</span>. Amines are a basic <span data-concept=\"functional-group\">functional group</span>. Amines and <span data-concept=\"carboxylic-acid\">carboxylic acids</span> can combine in a condensation reaction to form amides.</p>",
 "notes": "The Link to Learning on how DNA is packaged is left out. The section keeps as printed “a phosphate group (−PO₄³⁻) When new DNA” without its full stop, “one of which are proteins”, the glossary’s amine “bonded to one or more alkyl group”, the key to exercise fs-idp39423488 with “+ Cl” for Cl₂ and “CH₃CH(Cl)H(Cl)CH₃”, and “pyridium ion” in the image of the key to exercise fs-idm49015296.",
